 "approach": "I wrote a stdlib simulator (sim.py) of every rule in environment.md: channels, tip capacity, minimum volume, dead volume, overfill, layers, mix limits, and carryover traces where each touch leaves and picks up traces. I checked it with negative tests (negtests.py); each violation class fired correctly. The colleague's plan never stops on a hard rule, but at step 5 tips that touched samples go back into the reagent reservoir, leaving traces of sample_A1..H1. That is used_tip_contaminates_a_source. Steps 1-4 are clean, and step 4's mix of 50 uL in 100 uL is exactly half, so it counts. The corrected plan, 60 steps, repeats for each column 1-12: fresh tips, 40 uL reagent, dispense, mix 75 uL x3, drop. The simulator finds no errors or traces, and every well ends homogeneous with 60 uL sample + 40 uL reagent.",
 "doubts": "1) Step 4's mix sits exactly on the threshold (50 of 100 uL). With \"at least half\" it is valid; a grader using a strict \">\" would call step 4 mix_too_small. I used 75 uL to be safe either way. 2) \"Foreign\" is never defined. I treated reagent as allowed in assay wells, so the reagent trace left at step 3 is not contamination. 3) Traces carry no volume, so calling them \"puts a liquid where it does not belong\" is a reading, not stated. 4) The rack holds exactly 96 tips for 96 wells, leaving no slack. 5) The deck keys the reservoir as \"A1\", but the rules say to give no address."
}
